WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.990
Pour yourself a parsley soda because Season
2 of A Series of Unfortunate Events is an

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.429
entertainingly dark ride that's positively
jam-packed with easter eggs!

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:13.499
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm going to run through some very

00:00:13.499 --> 00:00:18.439
cool things you might have missed in the new
series including references, cameos, and connections

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:21.400
to TV shows, movies, and the original books.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.419
Obviously, spoilers ahead if you're not up-to-date
with Season 2.

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:29.929
At the start of the first episode, Mr Poe
mentions how he simply can't put down a book

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:33.780
called The Pony Party, which, if you look
at the jacket, seems to be the first in a

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.170
series of cheerful stories about 'the Luckiest
Kids in the World!'

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.149
written by Loney M Setnick.

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:44.200
However, in reality, there is no Pony Party
book, it's just a reversible cover for hardback

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.379
editions of Lemony Snicket: The Unauthorized
Autobiography, and Pony Party author Loney

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:53.390
M Setnick is an anagram of Lemony Snicket.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.969
If you've read the original books, you'll
know that Olivia Caliban is a rather different

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.089
character in the TV series.

00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:03.109
In the novels, she only appears in the 9th
book, The Carnivorous Carnival, where she's

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:07.070
known as the fortune-teller Madame Lulu and
she turns out to be the person who's been

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.720
giving Count Olaf information on where he
can find the Baudelaire orphans each time

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.300
they escape from him.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.790
And she's the one who reveals to Olaf that
Beverley, Elliot, and Chabo are actually Violet,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.070
Klaus, and Sunny!

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:24.030
However, in the TV show, Olivia Caliban pops
up at the start of Season 2 in The Austere

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.790
Academy episodes, where she's a librarian
who later joins up with Jacques Snicket to

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.650
try and help the Baudelaires, and later again
she takes on the alias of fortune-teller Madame

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.270
Lulu, which is a rotating position for any
VFD agent staying at the Carnival.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.090
By the way, the Caligari Carnival and Madame
Lulu's Caligari Cabinet get their names from

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.250
a silent horror movie called The Cabinet of
Dr Caligari.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.790
As Mr Poe drives the Baudelaires to their
next guardians in the Village of Fowl Devotees,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.720
he talks to them about Count Omar, which you
might think was just a slip of the tongue.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.590
"Thanks to the constant coverage in my wife's
newspaper, I'm sure Count Omar will be captured

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:00.750
in no time at all."

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.750
"Olaf."

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.160
"Yes, I meant to say Omar."

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.980
"Besides ..."
However, in the 7th book, The Vile Village,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:11.440
the Daily Punctilio newspaper gets Count Olaf's
name wrong, calling him Count Omar, which

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.030
leads the people living in the Village of
Fowl Devotees to repeatedly refer to Count

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.819
Olaf as Count Omar, much to the frustration
of the Baudelaires.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:25.049
Although the VFD villagers in the TV series
don't do this, Mr Poe does refer to Count

00:02:25.049 --> 00:02:29.040
Omar a couple of times in Part One and Two
of The Vile Village.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.250
"But you know that Count Omar was far from
innocent."

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.250
"Count Olaf."

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.250
"Yes, see?

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.250
Even the name gives the shivers."

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.969
"That bushy eyebrow and tattoo of that eye
on his ankle."

00:02:36.969 --> 00:02:42.219
And the Daily Punctilio also gets Olaf's name
wrong in several articles.

00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:46.310
In Part 2 of The Ersatz Elevator, there's
a clever call-back when, to gain access to

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.099
the "In Auction", Jacquelyn recycles the trick
that Count Olaf played on her in the first

00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:55.040
season by using the fake name he made up to
get an appointment with Mr Poe.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.870
"I have an appointment."

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.330
"Name?"

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:01.780
"Haircut."

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.230
"Haircut?"

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:08.689
"Yes... sica Haircut is my name."

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:10.040
"Yessica Haircut?"

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.040
"Yes."

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.040
"Yessica."

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.040
"Larry Your-Waiter."

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.500
"Did you get past the bouncer?"

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.500
"Not yet.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.159
He has to let me in."

00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:20.220
"I'm on the list."

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.250
"Yessica Haircut."

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.730
When Lemony Snicket is in the tunnels, there's
a sign for DENOUEMENT, which is a nod to the

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.110
Hotel Denouement, the main location of the
12th book, The Penultimate Peril, which will

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.870
be part of Season 3.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:36.109
The sign is also a reference to triplets Ernest,
Frank, and Dewey Denouement who own that hotel.

00:03:36.109 --> 00:03:40.209
And the word 'dénouement' is a literary term
meaning the final part of a film, play, or

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:42.560
story that brings together strands of the
plot.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.540
Also, when Klaus and Violet are searching
for the Snicket files at Heimlich Hospital,

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.139
there's a file labelled 'Slippery Slopes',
which is a nod to the 10th book, The Slippery

00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:54.519
Slope, which will be adapted in the first
episodes of Season 3.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:59.999
Of course, at the end of Season 2, the Baudelaires
also literally find themselves on a slippery

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:01.069
slope.

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:05.690
There's a lovely meta-moment in episode 9
when Neil Patrick Harris drops a reference

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:10.140
to his previous big TV show, How I Met Your
Mother, which ran for nine seasons and was

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.290
set in New York City.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.049
"If she's so good at fortune-telling, why
does she live here instead of having her own

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:16.049
show in the city?"

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:17.260
"I tried that for nine years.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.360
Look where it got me."

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.230
On top of that, Season 1 featured Harris's
How I Met Your Mother co-star Cobie Smulders

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.540
playing the Quagmire children's mother!

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.470
And Season 2 brings another reunion for Harris
and a former co-star when Olaf confronts Nathan

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.970
Fillion's Jacques Snicket in the Village of
Fowl Devotees.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.140
Harris and Fillion previously worked together
in Dr Horrible's Sing-Along Blog, where Harris

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.820
played an aspiring supervillain to Fillion's
superhero Captain Hammer.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.540
And they're not the only shows that Neil Patrick
Harris once starred in that get a hat-tip

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.570
this season.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:52.030
In episode 8, when Count Olaf meets Doctor
Faustus, who's actually Klaus in disguise,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.800
Olaf tells Faustus that he looks 'terribly
young to be a doctor'.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.350
Which is funny because as a young actor Harris's
breakthrough role was playing a teen genius

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:05.610
doctor in the comedy-drama, Doogie Howser,
MD, which ran from 1989 to 1993.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:10.350
Oh, and by the way, the name Doctor Faustus
is a literary reference to a 16th-century

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.680
English play about a man who makes a deal
with the devil.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.350
Esmé Squalor gets to wear some pretty memorable
outfits in the series, including one that

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.550
seems to draw inspiration from the villainous
Cruella de Vil.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:25.880
And, later, when Esmé tells Madame Lulu that
she was forced to 'make [her] own dress from

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:31.030
a few scraps of tent', and then the camera
reveals holes cut out of a tent, it's reminiscent

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.490
of Disney's Enchanted, where Giselle made
herself a dress out of a set of curtains,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.070
leaving an outline visible in the remaining
material.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.740
Now, you might think that's a stretch, but
Enchanted was produced by Barry Sonnenfeld

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.010
and he's the showrunner for A Series of Unfortunate
Events.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:51.620
Indeed, Barry Sonnenfeld also pops up several
times, including in a cameo as Aunt Josephine's

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.490
husband Ike Anwhistle; on a poster for 'Barry
the Pinhead' at the Caligari Carnival; and

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.810
he also provides the voice of Mr Tamerlane
during Mr Poe's phone conversation about The

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:01.810
Pony Party.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:02.810
"Poe, are you there?"

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:03.810
"Focus, Poe."

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:04.810
"What about the Baudelaires?"

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.810
"Ah, yes, the Baudelaires."

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:06.810
"Put down the book."

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.530
"I'll put this down."

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:14.430
And speaking of cameos, Neil Patrick Harris's
real-life husband, David Burtka, plays a rather

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:18.781
vocal audience member during the Carnivorous
Carnival episodes and they're joined in the

00:06:18.781 --> 00:06:22.900
season finale by their actual children Harper
and Gideon.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.890
The makers of A Series of Unfortunate Events
are obviously big fans of director Stanley

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:31.270
Kubrick's The Shining because they already
included a nod to the famous horror movie

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.970
in Season 1.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.830
"Here's Shammy!"

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.650
And they pay tribute to it again, not once
but twice in Season 2!

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:43.030
In Part One of the Hostile Hospital, administrator
Babs is wandering the hospital's corridors

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.430
in the dark when she's frightened by the two
identically-dressed White-Faced Women who

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.740
suddenly appear ahead of her.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.060
"Hello, Babs, come and play with us."

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.060
This scene is a clear hat-tip to the Grady
sisters in The Shining, who appear to young

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:01.190
Danny asking him to come and play with them
as he's tricycling along the hotel's corridors.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:05.770
In the second part of the Baudelaire's story
at Heimlich Hospital, the Henchperson of Indeterminate

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:14.110
Gender smashes down the door to a room where
Violet, Klaus and Sunny are hiding.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.610
"Here I am, Nurse Lucafont."

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:21.160
Which is a parody of Jack Nicholson's famous
line, 'Here's Johnny'.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.490
Now, what were your favourite easter eggs
and moments in the show?

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.750
And what are you most looking forward to in
season 3?

